WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.885
Firmware often processes input from
sensors, UART, SPI, I2C, CAN, USB, BLE,

2
00:00:03.965 --> 00:00:07.720
radio packets, local buttons, update
channels, debug commands, network messages,

3
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:08.646
or cloud services.

4
00:00:08.726 --> 00:00:10.630
Some of these inputs are clearly external.

5
00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.883
Others feel internal but still cross
reliability or trust boundaries.

6
00:00:13.963 --> 00:00:17.136
Input should be treated as unverified
until it is parsed and validated.

7
00:00:17.216 --> 00:00:19.860
A message can be syntactically valid and
still be too large,

8
00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:23.590
out of order, inconsistent with other
fields, unauthorized for the current mode,

9
00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.415
or unsafe for the current device state.

10
00:00:25.495 --> 00:00:27.082
Parsing confirms representation.

11
00:00:27.162 --> 00:00:29.224
Validation confirms meaning and
permission.

12
00:00:29.304 --> 00:00:32.742
Defensive Rust firmware uses bounded
buffers, explicit parser boundaries,

13
00:00:32.822 --> 00:00:36.365
length checks, framing checks, field
validation, state-machine validation,

14
00:00:36.445 --> 00:00:37.080
and timeouts.

15
00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:40.968
Device-facing parsing should avoid
panic-prone paths because invalid input should

16
00:00:41.048 --> 00:00:43.903
not make timing, availability, or device
state unpredictable.

17
00:00:43.983 --> 00:00:45.094
Protocol state matters.

18
00:00:45.174 --> 00:00:47.290
A command may only be valid after
provisioning.

19
00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:49.538
A field may be allowed only in a
maintenance mode.

20
00:00:49.618 --> 00:00:52.315
A sensor value may be plausible only
within a physical range.

21
00:00:52.395 --> 00:00:55.991
A firmware update command may require a
state transition before it can influence

22
00:00:56.071 --> 00:00:56.494
storage.

23
00:00:56.574 --> 00:00:59.113
These relationships should be validated
deliberately.

24
00:00:59.193 --> 00:01:02.684
A strong pattern is to convert valid input
into domain types after validation.

25
00:01:02.764 --> 00:01:06.201
Downstream code can then work with
reviewed meaning rather than raw bytes and

26
00:01:06.281 --> 00:01:06.863
primitives.

27
00:01:06.943 --> 00:01:08.582
The boundary becomes easier to test,

28
00:01:08.662 --> 00:01:12.100
and later tasks do not need to rediscover
which checks have already happened.
